Disciplinary system has 'flaws'.
Letter to the editor, pg. 12.

To the editor:
Dear University students:

As members of the Student Government Association Task Force
investigating the University disciplinary system, we feel it is
necessary to take this opportunity to inform the student community
about the latest developments regarding the disciplinary system.

Despite the tasks force's recommendations urging the University to
cease disciplinary proceedings against those students involved in the
May 5 protest and because of irregularities in the system that we have
discovered, the University is continuing with these hearings.

We feel that the students should be aware of the University's decision
and its refusal to investigate the problems that are associated with
this system.

We have discovered that the University student disciplinary system
contains several severe flaws that permit students to incriminate
themselves, denies them the right of counsel and places their future
in the hands of a committee that is unaware of proper legal procedure.
We feel that these flaws undermine the legitimacy of this committee to
be a fair disciplinary body and severely jeopardizes the future of
these students.

While the administration has admitted publicly that the disciplinary
system is flawed and believes that the system should be reviewed in
the future, they do not feel it necessary to stop these hearings so
that those students who are coming before the committee today and next
week can be tried under a more fair system.

Because of the importance of this issue, we urge all students to
attend the hearing that is being held today in the Student Services
Building and demonstrate your support for your fellow students.

Whether these students are guilty or not is not the main issue.
Currently the main issue is, and should always be, that students
should have the right to present their case in a system that is fair
for all those who are involved.

-- Student Government Association Task Force investigating the student
   disciplinary system

