I read the phrase "there are standards for reasonable conduct" as a
warning/threat of possible punitive measures.

dave@bradley.bradley.edu (David Vessell) writes:

>Freedom of speech in your forum is really a privilege, as is the case
>at most private universities that grant their students net access [.
>...]  However, every article you post has Northeastern University
>attached to it.It's more of a free press issue than a free speech
>issue; the university owns the 'press' (the computer used to access
>the net) and therefore enjoys editorial powers over its
>'contributors'.

While freedom of expression at private universities is not a legal
constitutional right, it is usually a legal contractual right. For
example, the Policy Statement On Student Rights And Responsibilities
of Northwestern U. (not to be confused with Northeastern) says:

"8. Students will be free from censorship in the publication and
   dissemination of their views as long as these are not
   represented as the views of Northwestern University."

(It, in my opinion, is also a moral right.)

Moreover, universities generally don't want or accept editorial
control of student publications. Not only would such control violate
academic freedom, it would increase the chances that the University is
legally liable for the publications.

Finally, it is a fundamental principle of academic freedom that
students and faculty may speak for themselves (even via univeristy
facilities). If Northeastern wants to make this principle explicit, it
can add a disclaimer to outgoing Netnews articles.

- Carl

